CRONIE
======

A git submodule (see docs/contrib.md), not a copy of the sources:

url    = https://github.com/cronie-crond/cronie.git
tag    = none: master, 35 commits after cronie-1.7.2 (cronie-1.7.2-35-g38773f4)
commit = 38773f4347bd86a5de089b21e924fa735ba45712
date   = 2026-10-18

Not a release tag, as an exception to docs/contrib.md: 1.7.2's
crontab(1) crashes on -r, -e and on replacing a crontab when ~/.cache
does not exist.  backup_crontab() then reaches umask(old_umask) with
old_umask unset, and clang drops that path, so the code runs into the
next function.  Upstream fixed it after 1.7.2 (fd56df9 makes ~/.cache,
855519a initializes the state), along with crontab parser fixes (reversed
and out-of-range ranges, @daily).  Move to cronie-1.7.3 (or later) when
it is released.

Nothing is deleted or modified in it: the build never writes into
contrib/cronie.  Not built: anacron (and obstack, which only anacron
uses), cronnext, the SELinux, Linux audit and inotify parts.

These sources are used in:
	usr.sbin/cron/cron	cron(8) (Cronie's crond), /usr/sbin/cron
				and /usr/sbin/crond
	usr.sbin/cron/crontab	crontab(1), crontab(5)

What configure would write is in usr.sbin/cron/include: config.h and
cron-paths.h (the BSD paths: /var/cron/tabs, /var/cron/allow and
/var/cron/deny).  execvpe(3), which this libc lacks, is in
usr.sbin/cron/cron/compat.c.  The manual pages get this system's paths
from usr.sbin/cron/cronie-man.sed when they are built.

When upgrading: check configure.ac for new HAVE_ and WITH_ macros and
src/Makemodule.am for new source files, then update this file's tag,
commit and date (tools/linux/check-submodules.sh compares the commit with
the one the tree records).

Cronie's licence: ISC and BSD (COPYING); cronie_common.c has no notice
of its own and comes under COPYING.  Not built and GPL: anacron
(COPYING.anacron), obstack (COPYING.obstack) and src/cronnext.c.
